            <div class="note red">
              <div class="note-label">📖 Best Practices Summary</div>

              <ul>
                <li><strong>✅ Use interfaces</strong> to define data shapes (API responses, component inputs, state)</li>
                <li><strong>✅ Let TypeScript infer types</strong> when obvious (<code>let name = "John"</code> instead of <code>let name: string = "John"</code>)</li>
                <li><strong>✅ Use union types</strong> for limited options (<code>type Status = 'loading' | 'success' | 'error'</code>)</li>
                <li><strong>✅ Use generics</strong> for reusable functions and components</li>
                <li><strong>✅ Avoid 'any'</strong> - it turns off type checking and defeats TypeScript's purpose</li>
                <li><strong>✅ Use optional properties (?)</strong> when fields may not exist</li>
                <li><strong>✅ Use readonly</strong> for properties that shouldn't change after creation</li>
                <li><strong>✅ Use const assertions</strong> for literal types (<code>as const</code>)</li>
                <li><strong>✅ Enable strict mode</strong> in tsconfig.json for maximum type safety</li>
