            <div class="note blue">
              <div class="note-label">📖 What is an MPA? (Traditional Websites)</div>
              <p><strong>MPA = Multi-Page Application</strong> - The traditional way websites work since the 1990s.</p>

              <p><strong>How MPA works:</strong></p>
              <ol>
                <li>You type a URL or click a link</li>
                <li>Browser sends request to server</li>
                <li>Server generates COMPLETE HTML page (all content, all styling)</li>
                <li>Browser downloads the HTML, CSS, JavaScript</li>
                <li>Browser renders the page from scratch (white screen flash)</li>
                <li>You click another link → Process repeats → Everything reloads again!</li>
              </ol>

              <p><strong>Real MPA Examples:</strong></p>
              <ul>
                <li>Old Amazon website (2005 era)</li>
                <li>Wikipedia (each article is a new HTML page)</li>
                <li>Traditional PHP websites</li>
                <li>WordPress sites (without caching plugins)</li>
              </ul>
            </div>

            <div class="note teal">
              <div class="note-label">📖 What is an SPA? (Angular Applications)</div>
              <p><strong>SPA = Single-Page Application</strong> - Modern approach that feels like a desktop app.</p>

              <p><strong>How SPA works:</strong></p>
              <ol>
                <li>User opens app for first time → Downloads ONE HTML file (index.html) + JavaScript bundle</li>
                <li>Angular takes control in the browser</li>
                <li>User clicks a link → Router intercepts click (stops browser from reloading)</li>
                <li>Router changes URL in address bar (using History API - no reload!)</li>
                <li>Router swaps components in &lt;router-outlet&gt;</li>
                <li>If data needed, fetches JSON from API (small, fast)</li>
                <li>Page updates instantly - no white screen, no full reload!</li>
              </ol>

              <p><strong>Real SPA Examples:</strong></p>
              <ul>
                <li>Gmail (inbox changes without page reload)</li>
                <li>Facebook (news feed updates, chat works while navigating)</li>
                <li>Google Maps (zoom, pan, search without reloads)</li>
                <li>YouTube (watch video while browsing other videos)</li>
                <li>Spotify Web Player (music keeps playing while you navigate)</li>
              </ul>
            </div>

            <div class="compare">
              <div class="compare-cell">
                <div class="label">📄 MPA — Multi Page Application</div>
                <p>Every URL → server sends a complete HTML page → browser repaints everything. Navigation = full reload. Examples: old PHP sites, server-rendered sites.</p>
              </div>
              <div class="compare-cell">
                <div class="label">⚡ SPA — Single Page Application (Angular)</div>
                <p>One HTML file loads once. After that, JavaScript swaps components in and out. Navigation = URL change + component swap. Zero full reloads.</p>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="note amber">
              <div class="note-label">📊 MPA vs SPA - Detailed Comparison</div>

              <table class="data-table">
                <thead>
                  <tr><th>Feature</th><th>MPA (Traditional)</th><th>SPA (Angular)</th></tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr>
                    <td>First page load</th>
                    <td>Fast (only this page's content)</th>
                    <td>Slower (downloads entire app framework)</th>
                  </tr>
                  <tr>
                    <td>Subsequent navigation</th>
                    <td>Slow (reloads everything)</th>
                    <td>Fast (just swaps components)</th>
                  </tr>
                  <tr>
                    <td>User experience</th>
                    <td>Jarring (white screen flashes)</th>
                    <td>Smooth (instant transitions)</th>
                  </tr>
                  <tr>
                    <td>Server load</th>
                    <td>Higher (generates HTML for each request)</th>
                    <td>Lower (serves static files + JSON only)</th>
                  </tr>
                  <tr>
                    <td>SEO (Search engines)</th>
                    <td>Excellent (ready-made HTML)</th>
                    <td>Needs extra work (SSR required)</th>
                  </tr>
                  <tr>
                    <td>State persistence</th>
                    <td>Lost between pages (unless stored)</th>
                    <td>Preserved while navigating</th>
                  </tr>
                  <tr>
                    <td>Offline support</th>
                    <td>Limited (each page needs network)</th>
                    <td>Better (can cache core app)</th>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

