          <!-- BLOCK 2: What "Single Page" Actually Means    -->
          <!-- ============================================= -->
          <div class="block">
            <h3>What "single page" actually means</h3>

            <div class="note purple">
              <div class="note-label">📖 The "Single Page" Misconception</div>
              <p><strong>"Single Page" does NOT mean:</strong> The app only has one screen or very little content.</p>
              <p><strong>"Single Page" actually means:</strong> The browser loads a SINGLE HTML file (index.html) once, and JavaScript handles all subsequent navigation WITHOUT reloading that page.</p>
              <p>You can have 1000 different screens (products, profiles, settings, checkout, etc.) all inside a single HTML file!</p>
            </div>

            <div class="note green">
              <div class="note-label">📖 The Bootstrap Process</div>
              <p>The browser only ever loads <code>index.html</code> once. That file loads your Angular JavaScript bundle. After that, Angular takes full control.</p>

              <pre data-lang="html"><span class="cm">&lt;!-- index.html - This is the ONLY HTML file the browser ever loads --&gt;</span>
      &lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;
      &lt;html&gt;
      &lt;head&gt;
        &lt;title&gt;My Angular App&lt;/title&gt;
      &lt;/head&gt;
      &lt;body&gt;
        <span class="cm">&lt;!-- Angular will replace this tag with your app --&gt;</span>
        &lt;app-root&gt;&lt;/app-root&gt;

        <span class="cm">&lt;!-- Angular JavaScript bundle - controls everything --&gt;</span>
        &lt;script src="main.js"&gt;&lt;/script&gt;
        &lt;script src="polyfills.js"&gt;&lt;/script&gt;
        &lt;script src="runtime.js"&gt;&lt;/script&gt;
      &lt;/body&gt;
      &lt;/html&gt;</pre>
            </div>

            <div class="note coral">
              <div class="note-label">📖 What Happens When You Click a Link in an SPA</div>

              <div class="steps">
                <div class="step">
                  <div class="step-num">1</div>
                  <div class="step-body">
                    <strong>Angular's Router intercepts the click</strong> - It prevents the browser's default navigation behavior (which would reload the page).
                  </div>
                </div>
                <div class="step">
                  <div class="step-num">2</div>
                  <div class="step-body">
                    <strong>The URL updates</strong> - Using the browser's History API, Angular changes what shows in the address bar (e.g., from /home to /products). No page reload happens!
                  </div>
                </div>
                <div class="step">
                  <div class="step-num">3</div>
                  <div class="step-body">
                    <strong>Router swaps components</strong> - Angular removes the current component from the <code>&lt;router-outlet&gt;</code> and creates/inserts the new component.
                  </div>
                </div>
                <div class="step">
                  <div class="step-num">4</div>
                  <div class="step-body">
                    <strong>Data fetching (if needed)</strong> - The new component asks a service for data. The service makes an HTTP request to the API, which returns JSON (small, fast). No HTML is fetched!
                  </div>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>

