          <!-- ============================================= -->
          <!-- BLOCK 5: SEO and Server-Side Rendering (SSR)  -->
          <!-- ============================================= -->
          <div class="block">
            <h3>SEO & Server-Side Rendering (Angular Universal)</h3>

            <div class="note purple">
              <div class="note-label">📖 The SEO Problem with SPAs</div>

              <p><strong>The Problem:</strong> Search engine crawlers (Google Bot) traditionally have trouble with JavaScript-rendered content.</p>

              <pre data-lang="text"><span class="cm">What Google Bot sees in a regular SPA:</span>
      &lt;app-root&gt;&lt;/app-root&gt;  <span class="cm">&lt;-- Empty! No content!</span&gt;

      <span class="cm">What Google Bot wants to see:</span>
      &lt;div&gt;
        &lt;h1&gt;Amazing Product - Only $99!&lt;/h1&gt;
        &lt;p&gt;This product will change your life...&lt;/p&gt;
        &lt;div class="reviews"&gt;★★★★☆ (128 reviews)&lt;/div&gt;
      &lt;/div&gt;</pre>
            </div>

            <div class="note teal">
              <div class="note-label">📖 Solution: Angular Universal (SSR)</div>

              <p><strong>SSR = Server-Side Rendering</strong> - The server generates the HTML BEFORE sending it to the browser.</p>

              <pre data-lang="text"><span class="cm">How SSR works:</span>
      1. User requests page → Server runs Angular on the server
      2. Server generates complete HTML with content
      3. Server sends HTML to browser (SEO-friendly!)
      4. Browser shows the page immediately
      5. Angular "hydrates" the page (adds interactivity)</pre>

              <pre data-lang="typescript"><span class="cm">// Angular Universal setup (simplified)</span>
      <span class="kw">import</span> { ngExpressEngine } <span class="kw">from</span> <span class="str">'@nguniversal/express-engine'</span>;

      <span class="kw">const</span> app = express();

      <span class="cm">// Render Angular app on the server</span>
      app.engine(<span class="str">'html'</span>, ngExpressEngine({
        bootstrap: AppServerModule
      }));

      app.get(<span class="str">'*'</span>, (req, res) => {
        res.render(<span class="str">'index'</span>, { req });  <span class="cm">// Generates HTML on server!</span>
      });</pre>
            </div>

            <div class="note amber">
              <div class="note-label">🎯 SPA vs SSR Comparison</div>

              <table class="data-table">
                <thead>
                  <tr><th>Feature</th><th>Regular SPA</th><th>SSR (Angular Universal)</th></tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr>
                    <td>First page load</th>
                    <td>Slow (download Angular first)</th>
                    <td>Fast (content ready immediately)</th>
                  </tr>
                  <tr>
                    <td>SEO</th>
                    <td>Poor (empty HTML sent)</th>
                    <td>Excellent (full HTML sent)</th>
                  </tr>
                  <tr>
                    <td>Social media sharing</th>
                    <td>Bad (no preview)</th>
                    <td>Good (rich previews)</th>
                  </tr>
                  <tr>
                    <td>Performance on slow networks</th>
                    <td>Poor (needs JS to work)</th>
                    </table>Good (HTML loads even if JS fails)</th>
                  </tr>
                  <tr>
                    <td>Server load</th>
                    <td>Very low (serves static files)</th>
                    <td>Higher (generates HTML per request)</th>
                  </tr>
                  <tr>
                    <td>Implementation complexity</th>
                    <td>Simple (standard Angular)</th>
                    <td>Complex (requires Universal setup)</th>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>

            <div class="note green">
              <div class="note-label">📖 Hybrid Approach: Prerendering</div>
              <p><strong>Prerendering</strong> = Generate HTML for SPECIFIC routes at build time (not runtime). Best for static pages like Home, About, Contact.</p>

              <pre data-lang="bash"><span class="cm"># Generate static HTML for specific routes</span>
      ng build --prerender-routes=/home,/about,/contact</pre>

              <p><strong>Use this when:</strong></p>
              <ul>
                <li>You have pages that rarely change (blog posts, product pages)</li>
                <li>You want great SEO without the complexity of full SSR</li>
                <li>Your dynamic content can be loaded client-side after page loads</li>
              </ul>
            </div>
          </div>

