                            <p><strong>flush()</strong> = saves buffered content to a section. target.content += buffer.join('\n') adds buffered lines with newlines, then buffer = [] resets it.</p>
                            <p><strong>for (const line of lines)</strong> = loops through every line in the markdown file in order.</p>
                            <p><strong>const match = line.match(/^(#{1,3})\s+(.+)/)</strong> = regular expression that matches headings. ^ = start of line, (#{1,3}) = 1-3 hash symbols, \s+ = spaces, (.+) = heading text.</p>
                            <p><strong>level = match[1].length</strong> = number of hash symbols (1, 2, or 3). title = match[2].trim() = heading text with whitespace removed.</p>
                            <p><strong>section = { id: this.slugify(title), title, level, content: '', children: [] }</strong> = creates new DocSection object. id is URL-safe version of title.</p>
                            <p><strong>flush(deepest())</strong> = BEFORE starting new section, save buffered content to PREVIOUS section.</p>
                            <p><strong>if (level === 1)</strong> = H1 heading → add to root array, set as currentH1, reset H2/H3.</p>
                            <p><strong>else if (level === 2)</strong> = H2 heading → add as child of current H1, set as currentH2, reset H3.</p>
                            <p><strong>else</strong> = H3 heading → add as child of current H2, set as currentH3.</p>
                            <p><strong>else { buffer.push(line) }</strong> = if line is NOT a heading, add it to buffer.</p>
                            <p><strong>flush(deepest())</strong> = after loop finishes, save any remaining buffered content to last section.</p>
                            <p><strong>slugify()</strong> = converts heading title to URL-friendly ID. toLowerCase() = lowercase, replace(/[^\w\s-]/g, '') = remove special characters, replace(/\s+/g, '-') = spaces to hyphens.</p>
                          </div>

                          <div class="file-explain">
                            <strong>How the buffer works:</strong> As we scan lines top-to-bottom, non-heading lines accumulate in <code>buffer[]</code>. When we hit the next heading, <code>flush()</code> joins those lines and assigns them as the previous section's <code>.content</code>. Then the buffer resets. This means each section's <code>.content</code> contains only the text between its heading and the next heading of the same or higher level.
                          </div>
                        </div>
                      </div>

          <div class="block">
            <h3>CopyCodeService — adds "Copy" buttons to code blocks</h3>
            <p>This service finds all code blocks on the page and adds a "Copy" button to each one. It's purely DOM manipulation — no state, no HTTP, just UI enhancement.</p>

            <div class="file-card">
              <div class="file-card-header">
                <span class="fname">copy-code.service.ts</span>
                <span class="fdesc">Adds copy buttons to &lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt; blocks</span>
              </div>
              <pre data-lang="typescript"><span class="dec">@Injectable</span>({ providedIn: <span class="str">'root'</span> })
      <span class="kw">export class</span> CopyCodeService {
        attachButtons(): <span class="kw">void</span> {
          <span class="comment">// Find all &lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt; blocks on the page</span>
          document.querySelectorAll(<span class="str">'pre code'</span>).forEach(block => {
            <span class="comment">// Get the parent &lt;pre&gt; element</span>
            <span class="kw">const</span> pre = block.parentElement!;

            <span class="comment">// Skip if button already exists (prevent duplicates)</span>
            <span class="kw">if</span> (pre.querySelector(<span class="str">'.copy-btn'</span>)) <span class="kw">return</span>;

            <span class="comment">// Create the button element</span>
            <span class="kw">const</span> btn = document.createElement(<span class="str">'button'</span>);
            btn.className = <span class="str">'copy-btn'</span>;
            btn.textContent = <span class="str">'Copy'</span>;

            <span class="comment">// Add click handler to copy code to clipboard</span>
            btn.addEventListener(<span class="str">'click'</span>, () => {
              navigator.clipboard.writeText(block.textContent ?? <span class="str">''</span>).then(() => {
                btn.textContent = <span class="str">'Copied!'</span>;
                <span class="comment">// Change back to "Copy" after 2 seconds</span>
                setTimeout(() => (btn.textContent = <span class="str">'Copy'</span>), <span class="num">2000</span>);
              });
            });

            <span class="comment">// Position button relative to the code block</span>
            pre.style.position = <span class="str">'relative'</span>;
            pre.appendChild(btn);
          });
        }
      }</pre>

              <div class="note blue">
                <div class="note-label">📖 Line by Line Explanation</div>
                <p><strong>@Injectable({ providedIn: 'root' })</strong> = decorator that tells Angular "this class can be injected into other classes". 'root' means Angular creates ONE shared instance (singleton) for the entire application.</p>
                <p><strong>export class CopyCodeService</strong> = exports this class so other files can import it.</p>
                <p><strong>attachButtons(): void</strong> = method that returns nothing (void). Called when new documentation section loads.</p>
                <p><strong>document.querySelectorAll('pre code')</strong> = browser API that finds ALL elements matching 'pre code' (every code block on the page). Returns a NodeList (like an array).</p>
                <p><strong>.forEach(block => { ... })</strong> = loops through each code block found. block is a DOM element representing the &lt;code&gt; tag.</p>
                <p><strong>const pre = block.parentElement!</strong> = gets the parent element of the &lt;code&gt; block (which is the &lt;pre&gt; wrapper). ! tells TypeScript "I know this exists, don't complain about null".</p>
                <p><strong>if (pre.querySelector('.copy-btn')) return</strong> = checks if a button with class 'copy-btn' already exists inside this &lt;pre&gt;. If yes, skip this block (prevents adding duplicate buttons when navigating back and forth).</p>
                <p><strong>const btn = document.createElement('button')</strong> = creates new HTML &lt;button&gt; element in memory (not on page yet).</p>
                <p><strong>btn.className = 'copy-btn'</strong> = sets CSS class for styling. btn.textContent = 'Copy' = sets button text.</p>
                <p><strong>btn.addEventListener('click', () => { ... })</strong> = adds an event listener that runs when button is clicked. Arrow function preserves 'this' context.</p>
                <p><strong>navigator.clipboard.writeText(block.textContent ?? '')</strong> = modern browser API that copies text to clipboard. block.textContent gets all text inside &lt;code&gt; block. ?? '' provides empty string fallback if textContent is null.</p>
                <p><strong>.then(() => { ... })</strong> = runs after copy succeeds. btn.textContent = 'Copied!' changes button text.</p>
                <p><strong>setTimeout(() => (btn.textContent = 'Copy'), 2000)</strong> = waits 2000 milliseconds (2 seconds), then changes button text back to 'Copy'.</p>
                <p><strong>pre.style.position = 'relative'</strong> = sets CSS position property. Required so the copy button can position itself absolutely inside the &lt;pre&gt; block.</p>
                <p><strong>pre.appendChild(btn)</strong> = adds the button to the page as the last child of the &lt;pre&gt; element.</p>
              </div>

              <div class="file-explain">
                <strong>Why a service for this?</strong> Copy buttons need to be added whenever new content loads (when user navigates to a new documentation section). Putting this logic in a service means it can be called from anywhere — DocViewerComponent calls it after rendering new content. Separating DOM manipulation into a service keeps components focused on data and routing.
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="block">
            <h3>DocsStateService — central data store for documentation</h3>
            <p>This service acts as the "single source of truth" for documentation data. It loads the README from GitHub once, parses it, and provides methods to query sections. All components get their data from here.</p>

            <div class="file-card">
              <div class="file-card-header">
                <span class="fname">docs-state.service.ts</span>
                <span class="fdesc">State management with BehaviorSubject</span>
              </div>
              <pre data-lang="typescript"><span class="dec">@Injectable</span>({ providedIn: <span class="str">'root'</span> })
      <span class="kw">export class</span> DocsStateService {
        <span class="kw">private</span> github = inject(<span class="tp">GithubService</span>);
        <span class="kw">private</span> parser = inject(<span class="tp">DocsParserService</span>);

        <span class="comment">// BehaviorSubject holds current value + emits to new subscribers</span>
        <span class="kw">private</span> sectionsSubject = <span class="kw">new</span> BehaviorSubject&lt;<span class="tp">DocSection</span>[]&gt;([]);
        <span class="kw">readonly</span> sections$ = this.sectionsSubject.asObservable();

        <span class="kw">private</span> loaded = <span class="kw">false</span>;

        <span class="comment">/** Load and parse the README from GitHub */</span>
        loadReadme(): <span class="kw">void</span> {
          <span class="kw">if</span> (this.loaded) <span class="kw">return</span>;  <span class="comment">// Prevent multiple loads</span>
          this.loaded = <span class="kw">true</span>;

          this.github.getReadme().pipe(
            map(md => this.parser.parse(md)),
            tap(sections => this.sectionsSubject.next(sections))
          ).subscribe();
        }

        <span class="comment">/** Find a section by id (searches flat + nested) */</span>
        getSectionById(id: <span class="tp">string</span>): <span class="tp">Observable</span>&lt;<span class="tp">DocSection</span> | <span class="kw">undefined</span>&gt; {
          <span class="kw">return</span> this.sections$.pipe(
            map(sections => this.findSection(sections, id))
          );
        }

        <span class="kw">private</span> findSection(sections: <span class="tp">DocSection</span>[], id: <span class="tp">string</span>): <span class="tp">DocSection</span> | <span class="kw">undefined</span> {
          <span class="kw">for</span> (<span class="kw">const</span> section <span class="kw">of</span> sections) {
            <span class="kw">if</span> (section.id === id) <span class="kw">return</span> section;
            <span class="kw">if</span> (section.children.length) {
              <span class="kw">const</span> found = this.findSection(section.children, id);
              <span class="kw">if</span> (found) <span class="kw">return</span> found;
            }
          }
          <span class="kw">return</span> <span class="kw">undefined</span>;
        }
      }</pre>

              <div class="note teal">
                <div class="note-label">📖 Line by Line Explanation</div>
                <p><strong>private github = inject(GithubService)</strong> = Angular's modern way to get a dependency. inject() asks Angular "give me the GithubService instance". No constructor needed.</p>
                <p><strong>private parser = inject(DocsParserService)</strong> = same pattern for the markdown parser service.</p>
                <p><strong>BehaviorSubject&lt;DocSection[]&gt;([])</strong> = special RxJS Observable that: (1) stores current value, (2) immediately emits that value to any new subscriber. Empty array [] is starting value.</p>
                <p><strong>readonly sections$ = this.sectionsSubject.asObservable()</strong> = exposes ONLY the Observable part (not the .next() method). $ suffix is convention for Observables. .asObservable() hides the ability to push new values.</p>
                <p><strong>private loaded = false</strong> = flag to prevent loading README multiple times. Starts false (not loaded yet).</p>
                <p><strong>loadReadme(): void</strong> = method called by ShellComponent when app starts.</p>
                <p><strong>if (this.loaded) return</strong> = if loaded already, exit immediately (prevents duplicate HTTP requests).</p>
                <p><strong>this.loaded = true</strong> = mark as loaded so future calls do nothing.</p>
                <p><strong>this.github.getReadme()</strong> = returns Observable&lt;string&gt; that emits the raw markdown from GitHub.</p>
                <p><strong>.pipe(map(md => this.parser.parse(md)))</strong> = transforms the Observable. map takes the markdown string, passes to parser.parse(), which returns DocSection[] array. Components receive the parsed array, not raw markdown.</p>
                <p><strong>.tap(sections => this.sectionsSubject.next(sections))</strong> = tap is like map but for side effects (doesn't change the value). .next(sections) pushes the parsed sections into the BehaviorSubject — all subscribers automatically receive the new value.</p>
                <p><strong>.subscribe()</strong> = triggers the Observable chain. Without subscribe(), nothing happens (Observables are lazy — they don't run until someone subscribes).</p>
                <p><strong>getSectionById(id: string): Observable&lt;DocSection | undefined&gt;</strong> = returns an Observable that will emit the section with matching ID (or undefined if not found).</p>
                <p><strong>return this.sections$.pipe( map(sections => this.findSection(sections, id)) )</strong> = when sections$ emits (when data loads), run findSection on the array and emit the result.</p>
                <p><strong>private findSection(sections, id)</strong> = recursive search function. Loops through top-level sections, checks each section's ID, then recursively searches children. Returns section if found, otherwise undefined.</p>
              </div>

              <div class="file-explain">
                <strong>Why BehaviorSubject?</strong> If a component subscribes BEFORE data loads, it needs to receive data automatically when it arrives. BehaviorSubject caches the last value and gives it to new subscribers immediately. Without this, components would need complex "is loaded?" logic.<br><br>
                <strong>Why separate sections$ and sectionsSubject?</strong> Exposing only sections$ (via .asObservable()) prevents other code from calling .next() and corrupting the data. Only the service itself can update the state — this is encapsulation.
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="block">
            <h3>FavoritesService — persists user's saved sections</h3>
            <p>Manages a list of favorite documentation sections. Stores them in localStorage so favorites persist across page refreshes. Uses BehaviorSubject for reactive UI updates.</p>

            <div class="file-card">
              <div class="file-card-header">
                <span class="fname">favorites.service.ts</span>
                <span class="fdesc">LocalStorage-backed favorites with reactive state</span>
              </div>
              <pre data-lang="typescript"><span class="dec">@Injectable</span>({ providedIn: <span class="str">'root'</span> })
      <span class="kw">export class</span> FavoritesService {
        <span class="kw">private</span> KEY = <span class="str">'devdocs_favorites'</span>;
        <span class="kw">private</span> favorites$ = <span class="kw">new</span> BehaviorSubject&lt;<span class="tp">Favorite</span>[]&gt;(this.load());

        getAll(): <span class="tp">Observable</span>&lt;<span class="tp">Favorite</span>[]&gt; {
          <span class="kw">return</span> this.favorites$.asObservable();
        }

        isFavorite(id: <span class="tp">string</span>): <span class="tp">boolean</span> {
          <span class="kw">return</span> this.favorites$.value.some(f => f.id === id);
        }

        toggle(section: <span class="tp">DocSection</span>): <span class="kw">void</span> {
          <span class="kw">const</span> current = this.favorites$.value;
          <span class="kw">const</span> exists = current.findIndex(f => f.id === section.id);

          <span class="kw">const</span> updated = exists >= <span class="num">0</span>
            ? current.filter(f => f.id !== section.id)          <span class="comment">// remove if exists</span>
            : [...current, {
                id: section.id,
                title: section.title,
                savedAt: <span class="tp">Date</span>.now()
              }];                                                <span class="comment">// add if not exists</span>

          this.favorites$.next(updated);
          <span class="kw">localStorage</span>.setItem(this.KEY, <span class="tp">JSON</span>.stringify(updated));
        }

        <span class="kw">private</span> load(): <span class="tp">Favorite</span>[] {
          <span class="kw">try</span> {
            <span class="kw">return</span> <span class="tp">JSON</span>.parse(<span class="kw">localStorage</span>.getItem(this.KEY) ?? <span class="str">'[]'</span>);
          }
          <span class="kw">catch</span> {
            <span class="kw">return</span> [];
          }
        }
      }</pre>

              <div class="note purple">
                <div class="note-label">📖 Line by Line Explanation</div>
                <p><strong>private KEY = 'devdocs_favorites'</strong> = string key used to store/fetch from localStorage. Must be unique across the app to avoid conflicts.</p>
                <p><strong>private favorites$ = new BehaviorSubject&lt;Favorite[]&gt;(this.load())</strong> = creates BehaviorSubject that loads existing favorites from localStorage as its initial value. this.load() runs once when service is created.</p>
                <p><strong>getAll(): Observable&lt;Favorite[]&gt;</strong> = returns Observable that components can subscribe to. Whenever favorites change, all subscribers receive the new array automatically.</p>
                <p><strong>return this.favorites$.asObservable()</strong> = returns read-only version. Prevents components from calling .next() directly.</p>
                <p><strong>isFavorite(id: string): boolean</strong> = synchronous method (not Observable). Checks if ID exists in current favorites array. this.favorites$.value accesses the current value synchronously.</p>
                <p><strong>.some(f => f.id === id)</strong> = array method that returns true if ANY element matches the condition. Stops looping once found.</p>
                <p><strong>toggle(section: DocSection): void</strong> = main method that adds section if not favorited, removes if already favorited.</p>
                <p><strong>const current = this.favorites$.value</strong> = gets current array (synchronous snapshot).</p>
                <p><strong>const exists = current.findIndex(f => f.id === section.id)</strong> = finds index of section in array. Returns -1 if not found, otherwise returns the position (0, 1, 2, etc.).</p>
                <p><strong>const updated = exists >= 0 ? ... : ...</strong> = ternary operator (if/else shorthand). If exists (index >= 0): filter OUT the section (creates new array without it). If not exists: spread operator ... creates copy of current array, then adds new favorite object.</p>
                <p><strong>{ id: section.id, title: section.title, savedAt: Date.now() }</strong> = creates Favorite object. savedAt timestamp (milliseconds since 1970) allows sorting by when added.</p>
                <p><strong>this.favorites$.next(updated)</strong> = pushes updated array into BehaviorSubject. All subscribers (like FavoritesComponent) automatically re-render.</p>
                <p><strong>localStorage.setItem(this.KEY, JSON.stringify(updated))</strong> = saves to browser's localStorage. JSON.stringify converts array to JSON string (localStorage only stores strings). This persists across page refreshes.</p>
                <p><strong>private load(): Favorite[]</strong> = loads favorites from localStorage when service initializes.</p>
                <p><strong>JSON.parse(localStorage.getItem(this.KEY) ?? '[]')</strong> = localStorage.getItem returns null if key doesn't exist. ?? '[]' provides empty array fallback. JSON.parse converts string back to JavaScript array.</p>
                <p><strong>try/catch</strong> = prevents app from crashing if localStorage data is corrupted or missing. Returns empty array as fallback.</p>
              </div>

              <div class="file-explain">
                <strong>Why BehaviorSubject for favorites?</strong> FavoritesComponent needs to update instantly when user clicks the favorite button. With BehaviorSubject, the component subscribes once and automatically re-renders when toggle() calls .next(). No manual "refresh list" calls needed.<br><br>
                <strong>Why synchronous isFavorite()?</strong> Checking if an item is favorited needs to happen synchronously (during render). BehaviorSubject's .value property provides immediate access without subscribing.
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

