              <p><strong>Observables - Data over time:</strong> Unlike a variable that holds a single value, an Observable emits values over time (like a stream).</p>

              <p><strong>Observable vs Regular variable:</strong></p>
              <ul>
                <li>Regular variable: <code>let name = "John"</code> - one value, now. If data changes later, you wouldn't know.</li>
                <li>Observable: <code>name$: Observable&lt;string&gt;</code> - emits values: "John" → then later "Jane" → then later "Bob". Subscribers get each update.</li>
              </ul>

              <p><strong>BehaviorSubject - Observable with memory:</strong> Regular Observable: new subscribers miss previous values. BehaviorSubject: new subscribers get the LATEST value immediately.</p>

              <pre data-lang="typescript"><span class="cm">// Create BehaviorSubject with initial value</span>
      <span class="kw">private</span> userSubject = <span class="kw">new</span> BehaviorSubject&lt;User&gt;(<span class="kw">null</span>);

      <span class="cm">// Expose as Observable (read-only to outside)</span>
      user$ = this.userSubject.asObservable();

      <span class="cm">// Update the value (anytime, anywhere)</span>
      updateUser(newUser: User) {
        this.userSubject.next(newUser);  <span class="cm">// All subscribers get this</span>
      }

      <span class="cm">// Subscribe in component</span>
      ngOnInit() {
        this.userService.user$.subscribe(user => {
          this.currentUser = user;  <span class="cm">// Gets current value immediately</span>
        });
      }</pre>

              <p><strong>The $ naming convention:</strong> By convention, variables ending with $ are Observables. <code>users$</code>, <code>currentUser$</code>, <code>isLoading$</code>. This tells other developers "this is a stream, you must subscribe to get values".</p>
